But that is not the case. You see, there are many parallels between unconditional self-esteem and the Buddhist conception of detachment.

583
01:13:25,160 --> 01:13:31,080
Let me read to you something that was written by the Buddhist nun, Tukten Chodron, and I quote.

584
01:13:32,920 --> 01:13:36,820
Ordinarily, we become very upset when we are criticized or insulted.

585
01:13:37,200 --> 01:13:42,780
We're angry when our possessions are stolen. We're jealous if someone else gets the promotion we wanted.

586
01:13:43,280 --> 01:13:46,040
We're proud of our looks or our slatic ability.

587
01:13:46,680 --> 01:13:54,880
By contrast, when we reach the state of detachment, our mind becomes clearer and more capable of enjoying things for what they are.

588
01:13:55,220 --> 01:14:02,520
We live more in the present, appreciating things as they are now, without fantasies about what they are or will be.

589
01:14:02,840 --> 01:14:08,700
We become less ego-sensitive to every action other people do in relation to us.

590
01:14:09,460 --> 01:14:14,800
Now, at the same time, detachment or unconditional self-esteem, which is the parallel that I'm drawing,

